= The Typed Imp Language Specification
:toc: left
:toclevels: 2
:sectnums:
:icons: font

Typed Imp is a small typed imperative language. Its syntax covers
integer and boolean literals, variables, arithmetic and boolean
operators, sequencing, conditionals, `while` loops, and typed variable
declarations. The static semantics assigns types to expressions and
commands under a typing context; the dynamic semantics evaluates
expressions and executes commands under an environment that maps
variables to values.

== Syntax

The abstract syntax of Typed Imp distinguishes types, literals,
variables, expressions, commands, and whole programs.

[[type]]
[[literal]]
[[id]]
[[expr]]
[[command]]
[[prog]]
[source,bison]
----
syntax type = 
   | INT
   | BOOL
   | {type -> type}

syntax literal = 
   | {_NUM int}
   | {_BOOL bool}

syntax id = 
   | {_ID text}

syntax expr = 
   | literal
   | id
   | {! expr}
   | {expr + expr}
   | {expr <= expr}
   | {expr && expr}
   | {FUN `({type id}) -> type `{expr}}
   | {expr `(expr)}

syntax command = 
   | SKIP
   | {type id = expr}
   | {id = expr}
   | {IF expr THEN command ELSE command END}
   | {WHILE expr DO command END}
   | {command ; command}

syntax prog = command
----

== Auxiliary definitions

Lookup over an association list is shared by the static and dynamic
semantics. Given a list of `(key, value)` pairs, it returns the first
value bound to the requested key.

